Me too, but once I get there, it is my favorite part of the
game so far, even when I get some stupid floating eye
trapped behind a boulder between it and the hole, and have
to go back to the main dungeon to find some way of killing a
floating eye beyond a boulder without breaking the boulder
or squeezing past it.

And it is _so_ satisfying when you do get a "perfect"
solution, it would be a shame to have Sokoban go away.

xanthian.

[Now if someone would just do a 3D Sokoban, where instead of
giving a luck penalty, jumping down the holes was the only
way to solve some levels...]

Umm, by the way, I went web crawling looking at the "Sokoban
generators" someone mentioned here, and at least the ones I
found that included descriptions of what exactly they do,
aren't at all impressive. The user has to design the level
wall and barrier layouts, _then_ the generator will lay out
the boulders, in a way to cause the solution to be the
longest possible one, if desired, which is nice, but sort of
misses the point.

That's not anything close to designing a whole Sokoban level
puzzle, since the level design and the boulder layout design
must interact with one another to make a puzzle interesting,
it seems to me.

On the other hand, there are _lots_ of freely available
already designed Sokoban levels on the Net that might work
just to expand what NetHack has now, if that were the goal.
You wouldn't have to have all that many optional levels for
each Sokoban level to make that part of the game effectively
brand new for every player, every time.

There are also automatic Sokoban _solvers_, quite a bit
easier of a problem, so cheating is still possible if you
want to deprive yourself of the fun of figuring out a level
on your own.



